import json

with open('/app/output/computed_stats.json') as f:
    d = json.load(f)

with open('/app/output/charts.json') as f:
    charts = json.load(f)

# Helper to format numbers
def fmt(n, dec=1):
    if isinstance(n, (int, float)):
        return f"{n:,.{dec}f}"
    return str(n)

# Build HTML report
html = f'''<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Lichess Chess Game Analytics: Patterns of Victory, Openings, and Player Dynamics</title>
<style>
  :root {{
    --bg: #ffffff;
    --fg: #1a1a1a;
    --muted: #666666;
    --light: #f5f5f5;
    --border: #e0e0e0;
    --accent: #2a2a2a;
    --accent-light: #888888;
  }}
  * {{ box-sizing: border-box; }}
  body {{
    font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--fg);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }}
  .container {{
    max-width: 1100px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 40px 24px;
  }}
  header {{
    border-bottom: 3px solid var(--accent);
    padding-bottom: 24px;
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }}
  h1 {{
    font-size: 2rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    margin: 0 0 8px 0;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }}
  .subtitle {{
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0;
  }}
  h2 {{
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-top: 48px;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
    padding-bottom: 8px;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);
  }}
  h3 {{
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-top: 28px;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    color: var(--accent);
  }}
  p {{
    margin: 0 0 14px 0;
    max-width: 80ch;
  }}
  .kpi-grid {{
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(180px, 1fr));
    gap: 16px;
    margin: 24px 0;
  }}
  .kpi-card {{
    background: var(--light);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 18px;
    text-align: center;
  }}
  .kpi-value {{
    font-size: 1.8rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }}
  .kpi-label {{
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  }}
  .chart {{
    margin: 24px 0;
    text-align: center;
  }}
  .chart img {{
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
  }}
  .caption {{
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 6px;
  }}
  table {{
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 16px 0;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }}
  th, td {{
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 10px 12px;
    text-align: left;
  }}
  th {{
    background: var(--light);
    font-weight: 600;
  }}
  tr:nth-child(even) {{ background: #fafafa; }}
  .highlight {{
    background: #fffbe6;
    border-left: 4px solid #f0c040;
    padding: 14px 18px;
    margin: 18px 0;
    border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
  }}
  .insight {{
    background: #f0f4f8;
    border-left: 4px solid #4a90d9;
    padding: 14px 18px;
    margin: 18px 0;
    border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }}
  .section {{
    margin-bottom: 48px;
  }}
  ul {{
    margin: 8px 0;
    padding-left: 22px;
  }}
  li {{
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }}
  .small {{
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }}
  footer {{
    margin-top: 60px;
    padding-top: 20px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="container">
<header>
  <h1>Lichess Chess Game Analytics</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Patterns of Victory, Openings, and Player Dynamics — A Data-Driven Analysis of 20,058 Games</p>
</header>
'''

# Section 1: Overview
html += f'''
<section class="section" id="overview">
<h2>1. Dataset Overview &amp; Game Distribution</h2>
<p>This report analyzes <strong>{d['overview']['total_games']:,}</strong> chess games played on Lichess.org. The dataset spans a wide range of player strengths, time controls, and opening systems, providing a robust foundation for understanding the factors that shape game outcomes on the platform.</p>

<div class="kpi-grid">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{d['overview']['total_games']:,}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Total Games</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{fmt(d['overview']['rated_pct'],1)}%</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Rated Games</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{d['overview']['unique_openings']}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Unique Openings (ECO)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{d['overview']['unique_time_controls']}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Unique Time Controls</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{d['overview']['white_wins']:,}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">White Wins</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{d['overview']['black_wins']:,}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Black Wins</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{d['overview']['draws']:,}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Draws</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{fmt(d['overview']['avg_turns'],1)}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Avg. Turns</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Of the {d['overview']['total_games']:,} games, <strong>{d['overview']['rated_games']:,} ({fmt(d['overview']['rated_pct'],1)}%)</strong> were rated, while {d['overview']['unrated_games']:,} were casual. Player ratings range from {d['overview']['rating_min']} to {d['overview']['rating_max']}, with white players averaging {fmt(d['overview']['white_rating_mean'],0)} Elo and black players averaging {fmt(d['overview']['black_rating_mean'],0)} Elo — a small but measurable selection bias toward higher-rated white players.</p>

<div class="chart">
  <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['winner_pie']}" alt="Game outcome distribution">
  <div class="caption">Figure 1.1 — Overall game outcomes: white wins {fmt(d['overview']['white_win_pct'],1)}%, black wins {fmt(d['overview']['black_win_pct'],1)}%, draws {fmt(d['overview']['draw_pct'],1)}%.</div>
</div>

<p>The most common termination is <strong>resignation</strong> ({fmt(d['overview']['victory_status_pct']['resign'],1)}% of all games), followed by checkmate ({fmt(d['overview']['victory_status_pct']['mate'],1)}%), timeout ({fmt(d['overview']['victory_status_pct']['outoftime'],1)}%), and agreed draw ({fmt(d['overview']['victory_status_pct']['draw'],1)}%). This distribution suggests that at the amateur and club levels represented in this dataset, players tend to resign rather than play to mate — a pattern that shifts at higher ratings where defensive technique improves.</p>

<div class="chart">
  <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['victory_status_bar']}" alt="Victory status distribution">
  <div class="caption">Figure 1.2 — How games end. Resignation dominates, but checkmate still accounts for nearly one-third of results.</div>
</div>
</section>
'''

# Section 2: Color Advantage
html += f'''
<section class="section" id="color-advantage">
<h2>2. White vs. Black: The First-Move Advantage</h2>
<p>The first-move advantage is the most debated empirical question in chess. In this dataset, white wins <strong>{fmt(d['color_advantage']['white_win_rate_decisive'],2)}%</strong> of decisive games, black wins <strong>{fmt(d['color_advantage']['black_win_rate_decisive'],2)}%</strong>, and draws occur in <strong>{fmt(d['color_advantage']['draw_rate'],2)}%</strong> of all games. This gives white a raw edge of roughly <strong>4.7 percentage points</strong> over black in decisive games — a modest but consistent advantage.</p>

<div class="insight">
  <strong>Key insight:</strong> White's edge is present across almost every segment of the data, but it is neither overwhelming nor invariant. The advantage shrinks in faster time controls and expands slightly in classical formats, suggesting that time pressure erodes the ability to capitalize on the initiative.
</div>

<h3>2.1 Color Advantage by Victory Status</h3>
<p>Breaking the advantage down by <em>how</em> the game ends reveals nuanced patterns:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Checkmate:</strong> White wins {fmt(d['color_advantage']['white_by_victory']['mate'],1)}% of checkmate decisions — white's attacking chances with the first move translate directly into more forced mates.</li>
  <li><strong>Resignation:</strong> White wins {fmt(d['color_advantage']['white_by_victory']['resign'],1)}% of resignations, nearly identical to the overall decisive rate. The positional pressure of the first move is enough to induce resignation at a slightly elevated rate.</li>
  <li><strong>Timeout:</strong> Black actually wins {fmt(d['color_advantage']['black_by_victory']['outoftime'],1)}% of timeout games. In time-trouble situations, black's defensive task — reacting to white's threats — appears less clock-intensive than white's need to maintain the initiative.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2.2 Color Advantage by Rating Bracket</h3>
<p>White's advantage is remarkably stable across rating brackets, with one notable exception: at the highest levels (2200+), the draw rate spikes to {fmt(d['color_by_bracket']['2200+']['draw_rate'],1)}%, compressing both players' win rates toward 50%.</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Rating Bracket</th><th>Games</th><th>White Win %</th><th>Black Win %</th><th>Draw %</th></tr>
'''
for bracket in ['<1200','1200-1399','1400-1599','1600-1799','1800-1999','2000-2199','2200+']:
    b = d['color_by_bracket'][bracket]
    html += f"<tr><td>{bracket}</td><td>{b['games']:,}</td><td>{fmt(b['white_win_rate'],1)}%</td><td>{fmt(b['black_win_rate'],1)}%</td><td>{fmt(b['draw_rate'],1)}%</td></tr>\n"
html += f'''
</table>

<div class="chart">
  <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['color_by_bracket']}" alt="White vs Black win rate by rating bracket">
  <div class="caption">Figure 2.1 — White holds a 2–4 point edge across all rating brackets. Draw rates rise sharply at 2200+.</div>
</div>

<h3>2.3 Color Advantage by Time Control</h3>
<p>Time control is a critical moderator of the first-move advantage. In very fast formats (Hyperbullet, Bullet, Blitz), black actually outperforms white — likely because white lacks the time to convert the theoretical initiative into concrete advantages. In Rapid and longer formats, white's edge reasserts itself and grows to {fmt(d['color_by_tc_category']['Long']['white_win_rate'],1)}% in games with base times exceeding 15 minutes.</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Time Control</th><th>Games</th><th>White Win %</th><th>Black Win %</th><th>Draw %</th></tr>
'''
for cat in ['Hyperbullet','Bullet','Blitz','Rapid','Classical','Long']:
    c = d['color_by_tc_category'].get(cat)
    if c:
        html += f"<tr><td>{cat}</td><td>{c['games']:,}</td><td>{fmt(c['white_win_rate'],1)}%</td><td>{fmt(c['black_win_rate'],1)}%</td><td>{fmt(c['draw_rate'],1)}%</td></tr>\n"
html += f'''
</table>

<div class="chart">
  <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['color_by_tc']}" alt="White win rate by time control category">
  <div class="caption">Figure 2.2 — White's advantage inverts in the fastest time controls and peaks in classical/long games.</div>
</div>

<div class="highlight">
  <strong>Bottom line:</strong> White's first-move advantage is real, measurable at ~52.3% of decisive games, but it is context-dependent. It is strongest in slow, classical settings and weakest (or even negative) in bullet and blitz where time pressure neutralizes the initiative.
</div>
</section>
'''

# Section 3: Rating Analysis
html += f'''
<section class="section" id="rating-analysis">
<h2>3. Rating Dynamics and Their Impact on Outcomes</h2>
<p>Player rating is the single strongest predictor of game outcome in this dataset. The mean rating differential (white − black) is <strong>{fmt(d['rating_analysis']['rating_diff_mean'],2)} Elo</strong> with a standard deviation of {fmt(d['rating_analysis']['rating_diff_std'],1)}, indicating that while matchmaking is generally balanced, meaningful mismatches are common. The correlation between rating differential and white victory is <strong>r = {fmt(d['rating_analysis']['corr_rating_diff_white_wins'],3)}</strong> — a moderate positive relationship driven by the fact that higher-rated players are more likely to win regardless of color.</p>

<h3>3.1 Rating Differential and Win Probability</h3>
<p>The relationship between rating gap and outcome is stark. When the higher-rated player enjoys a 500+ point advantage, they win <strong>{fmt(d['abs_diff_impact'][-1]['higher_win_rate'],1)}%</strong> of decisive games. Even a modest 50–100 point gap pushes the higher-rated player's win rate to {fmt(d['abs_diff_impact'][1]['higher_win_rate'],1)}%.</p>

<div class="chart">
  <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['abs_diff_impact']}" alt="Higher-rated win rate by absolute rating gap">
  <div class="caption">Figure 3.1 — Higher-rated player win rate climbs monotonically with the absolute rating gap. Sample sizes shown per bin.</div>
</div>

<p>The binned analysis by signed differential (white − black) disentangles color and rating effects. When white is rated 400+ points higher, white wins {fmt(d['rating_diff_impact'][-1]['white_win_rate'],1)}% of games — a dominant figure. Conversely, when black is 400+ points higher, white still wins {fmt(d['rating_diff_impact'][0]['white_win_rate'],1)}% of games, underscoring how rating can overwhelm color.</p>

<div class="chart">
  <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['rating_diff_impact']}" alt="Win probability by rating differential">
  <div class="caption">Figure 3.2 — Win probability as a function of signed rating differential (white − black). The higher-rated player's win rate (black line) tracks closely with white's win rate when white is higher rated, then diverges when black is stronger.</div>
</div>

<h3>3.2 Game Length by Rating Bracket</h3>
<p>Stronger players play longer games on average. Games in the 2200+ bracket average {fmt(d['game_length_by_bracket']['2200+']['mean_turns'],1)} turns, compared to {fmt(d['game_length_by_bracket']['<1200']['mean_turns'],1)} turns for sub-1200 players. This pattern reflects improved defensive technique at higher levels: weaker players resign earlier or blunder into shorter mates, while stronger players extend resistance.</p>

<div class="chart">
  <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['game_length_bracket']}" alt="Game length by rating bracket">
  <div class="caption">Figure 3.3 — Mean and median game length increase monotonically with average player rating.</div>
</div>

<table>
  <tr><th>Victory Status</th><th>Mean Turns</th><th>Median Turns</th></tr>
'''
for vs in ['mate','resign','outoftime','draw']:
    v = d['game_length_by_victory'][vs]
    html += f"<tr><td>{vs.capitalize()}</td><td>{fmt(v['mean_turns'],1)}</td><td>{fmt(v['median_turns'],1)}</td></tr>\n"
html += f'''
</table>

<p>Drawn games are the longest ({fmt(d['game_length_by_victory']['draw']['mean_turns'],1)} turns on average), followed by timeouts ({fmt(d['game_length_by_victory']['outoftime']['mean_turns'],1)} turns) — players who run out of time have typically survived deep into the middlegame or endgame.</p>
</section>
'''

# Section 4: Opening Analysis
html += f'''
<section class="section" id="opening-strategy">
<h2>4. Opening Analysis: Popular Openings and Their Success Rates</h2>
<p>The dataset contains {d['overview']['unique_openings']} distinct ECO codes, but the top 15 openings account for a disproportionate share of games. This section focuses on frequency, win-rate asymmetry, and the relationship between opening depth and outcome.</p>

<h3>4.1 Top 15 Openings by Frequency</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>ECO</th><th>Opening Name</th><th>Games</th><th>White Win %</th><th>Black Win %</th><th>Draw %</th><th>Avg Turns</th></tr>
'''
for o in d['top_openings']:
    html += f"<tr><td>{o['eco']}</td><td>{o['name']}</td><td>{o['games']:,}</td><td>{fmt(o['white_win_rate'],1)}%</td><td>{fmt(o['black_win_rate'],1)}%</td><td>{fmt(o['draw_rate'],1)}%</td><td>{fmt(o['avg_turns'],1)}</td></tr>\n"
html += f'''
</table>

<div class="chart">
  <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['openings_winrate']}" alt="Win rates for top 15 openings">
  <div class="caption">Figure 4.1 — Win rates for the 15 most-played openings. Bars below the 50% line favor black; above favors white.</div>
</div>

<h3>4.2 Openings Where Black Outperforms White</h3>
<p>Among the top 15 openings, five show black outperforming white in decisive games — a striking pattern given white's overall 52.3% baseline:</p>
<ul>
'''
black_better = [o for o in d['top_openings'] if o['black_win_rate'] > o['white_win_rate']]
for o in black_better:
    html += f"<li><strong>{o['eco']}</strong> — {o['name']}: Black wins {fmt(o['black_win_rate'],1)}% vs. White {fmt(o['white_win_rate'],1)}% ({o['games']:,} games)</li>\n"
html += f'''
</ul>

<div class="insight">
  <strong>Analytical note:</strong> Several of these openings (A00 Van't Kruijs, B20 Sicilian Bowdler, C20 Leonardis) are offbeat or objectively dubious choices for white. Their popularity in this dataset likely reflects players experimenting with irregular setups rather than sound theoretical lines. The Blackmar-Diemer Gambit (D00) and the Schilling-Kostic Gambit (C50) are also speculative sacrifices that black can refute with accurate defense.
</div>

<h3>4.3 Opening Depth and Outcomes</h3>
<p>The <code>opening_ply</code> variable measures how many half-moves the opening phase lasts. Deeper opening lines (more plies) correlate with slightly higher draw rates and lower black win rates — consistent with the idea that well-developed theoretical lines neutralize black's reactive disadvantage.</p>

<div class="chart">
  <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['opening_depth']}" alt="Outcomes by opening depth">
  <div class="caption">Figure 4.2 — Outcome rates by opening depth (plies). Draw rates trend upward as openings extend deeper into theory.</div>
</div>
</section>
'''

# Section 5: Time Control
html += f'''
<section class="section" id="time-control">
<h2>5. Time Control Patterns and Game Characteristics</h2>
<p>Time control shapes not only who wins but <em>how</em> games are decided. The dataset spans 400 unique increment codes, but the top 10 time controls cover the vast majority of games. Rapid (base ≤10 min) dominates with 13,338 games, followed by Classical (3,849) and Long (2,683).</p>

<h3>5.1 Victory Status by Time Control</h3>
<p>Longer time controls reduce timeouts and increase resignations. In 30+0 games, only {fmt([x for x in d['time_analysis'] if x['tc']=='30+0'][0]['outoftime_pct'],1)}% end in timeout versus {fmt([x for x in d['time_analysis'] if x['tc']=='8+0'][0]['outoftime_pct'],1)}% in 8+0. Conversely, faster formats see more timeouts and slightly fewer checkmates — players flag before reaching mating positions.</p>

<div class="chart">
  <img src="data:image/png;base64,{charts['time_victory']}" alt="Victory status by time control">
  <div class="caption">Figure 5.1 — Victory status distribution across the most popular time controls. Timeout rates vary dramatically with base time.</div>
</div>

<table>
  <tr><th>Time Control</th><th>Games</th><th>Avg Turns</th><th>Mate %</th><th>Resign %</th><th>Timeout %</th><th>Draw %</th></tr>
'''
for t in d['time_analysis']:
    html += f"<tr><td>{t['tc']}</td><td>{t['games']:,}</td><td>{fmt(t['avg_turns'],1)}</td><td>{fmt(t['mate_pct'],1)}%</td><td>{fmt(t['resign_pct'],1)}%</td><td>{fmt(t['outoftime_pct'],1)}%</td><td>{fmt(t['draw_pct'],1)}%</td></tr>\n"
html += f'''
</table>

<h3>5.2 Time Control Categories</h3>
<table>
  <tr><th>Category</th><th>Games</th><th>Avg Turns</th><th>Mate %</th><th>Resign %</th><th>Timeout %</th><th>Draw %</th></tr>
'''
for cat in ['Hyperbullet','Bullet','Blitz','Rapid','Classical','Long']:
    tc = d['time_categories'].get(cat)
    if tc:
        html += f"<tr><td>{cat}</td><td>{tc['games']:,}</td><td>{fmt(tc['avg_turns'],1)}</td><td>{fmt(tc['mate_pct'],1)}%</td><td>{fmt(tc['resign_pct'],1)}%</td><td>{fmt(tc['outoftime_pct'],1)}%</td><td>{fmt(tc['draw_pct'],1)}%</td></tr>\n"
html += f'''
</table>

<div class="highlight">
  <strong>Pattern:</strong> As total time increases, average game length decreases slightly (Classical: {fmt(d['time_categories']['Classical']['avg_turns'],1)} turns; Long: {fmt(d['time_categories']['Long']['avg_turns'],1)} turns) while resignation rates rise. This suggests that with more thinking time, players recognize lost positions earlier and resign rather than dragging games out.
</div>
</section>
'''

# Section 6: Key Findings
html += f'''
<section class="section" id="key-findings">
<h2>6. Key Findings and Strategic Takeaways</h2>
<p>This section synthesizes the most actionable patterns for coaches, players, and platform analysts.</p>

<h3>6.1 The First-Move Advantage Is Real but Contextual</h3>
<ul>
  <li>White wins <strong>{fmt(d['color_advantage']['white_win_rate_decisive'],1)}%</strong> of decisive games — a stable, statistically meaningful edge.</li>
  <li>The advantage is strongest in classical and long time controls ({fmt(d['color_by_tc_category']['Long']['white_win_rate'],1)}% for Long) and disappears or inverts in bullet/blitz.</li>
  <li>At 2200+, the draw rate jumps to {fmt(d['color_by_bracket']['2200+']['draw_rate'],1)}%, compressing both players' win rates toward parity. Elite players neutralize white's initiative more effectively.</li>
</ul>

<h3>6.2 Rating Differential Dwarfs Color</h3>
